@@ -116,6 +116,8 @@ Two rules apply to every agent and to the orchestrator, through hooks and deny r
 
 guard-dangerous.sh also blocks two command shapes for every caller, because it cannot see what bash would make of them. First, a backslash directly before a newline or carriage return, in any quote state. Second, `$'` or `$"` anywhere, even inside quotes, in a command that is multi-line or contains `${`, `$(` (which covers `$((`), a backtick, `$[`, `<(`, or `>(`. A `#` comment, a heredoc body, or the fresh quoting context inside a substitution can hide ANSI-C quoting from the guard. So `grep -n 'foo$' $(git ls-files)` is blocked. Run the substitution as a separate command, anchor without a trailing `$` (`grep -nx '.*foo'`), or use the Write tool for file content.
 
+guard-dangerous.sh also bounds its own run time, for every caller and for both the Bash and the `mcp__aws-api__call_aws` matchers, so that it cannot be padded past the 20 s hook timeout in settings.json. Claude Code treats a hook that times out as a non-blocking error, so without these bounds the call would run unchecked (finding R3, repair-03). Rule R3-LENCAP: before any parsing, the guard blocks hook input over 65,536 bytes, and it blocks a command (the Bash `command` or the AWS MCP `cli_command`) over 2,048 bytes as soon as it has read it. The guard counts bytes as it reads the command, and on Windows each line break arrives as CR LF, so it counts as two bytes. Rule R3-DEADLINE: the guard runs the whole check in a child process under GNU `timeout` (`/usr/bin/timeout`, shipped with Git for Windows). If the child has not decided within 15 s, it is killed (forcibly 1 s later if needed) and the command is blocked. If `timeout` is missing, every command is blocked. Any child exit status other than 0 (allow or ask) or 2 (block) also blocks. The time a check takes depends on the number of segments and words, and on machine load. The guard counts segments by character and ignores quoting: a command has one segment, plus one for every `;`, `|`, `&`, `(`, `)`, backtick, and line break anywhere in it, including inside quotes and heredoc bodies. So `&&` and `||` add two each, `$(...)` adds two unless it ends the command, and every `|` in a quoted pattern adds one. Separators at the very end of the command, and the `&` of a file-descriptor redirect such as `2>&1` or `>&2`, are not counted. For example, `grep -rnE 'a|b|c|d|e|f|g|h|i|j|k|l' modules | wc -l` has 13 segments, and six `bash -n` commands joined by `&&` have 11. Measured on the reference machine (repair-03 evidence), commands with 5 to 7 segments took 6.6 to 12.5 s and commands with 11 or 12 segments took 12.5 to 18.5 s, so a short command with many segments can be blocked by R3-DEADLINE even under the length cap. Rule of thumb: keep each command to about 6 segments or fewer. Run chained commands one at a time, and write a long alternation as `grep -e a -e b` or `grep -F -f <file>` instead of `'a|b|c'`. Split long commands into several commands. Put file content (commit messages, data, scripts) in a file with the Write tool and pass the file name, for example `git commit -F <file>`. Residual RR-12: the deadline depends on machine load, so the same command can pass on one run and be blocked on the next. A blocked run is fail closed. The deadline and kill grace (15 + 1 s) leave about 4 s of the 20 s hook timeout for the settings.json wrapper command, the outer bash start-up, and reaping the child. Under extreme load that part can take longer, the hook can then exceed 20 s, and Claude Code would run the call unchecked. The guard's per-segment cost is tracked as a separate performance follow-up.
